          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes is a condition where the body's tissues do not respond properly to insulin, leading to elevated blood glucose levels. It develops gradually over the years, often starting with a state called prediabetes. Many people do not show symptoms at diagnosis (source: General clinical reference on type 2 diabetes).\n\nFirst-line therapy for managing type 2 diabetes typically includes lifestyle changes like diet and physical activity. Additionally, medications such as SGLT-2 inhibitors may be commonly used, with targets for blood glucose control usually set at an HbA1c below 7 percent for many adults (source: National diabetes association standards of care).\n\nThe ICD-10 code for type 2 diabetes is E11 (source: ICD-10 coding system). \n\nI could not cover specific treatment plans or medication details beyond what is generally suggested. For personalized advice, discussing with a healthcare provider is essential.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
